{%- comment -%}
  Explainer figure: the two ideas in Cleaner Adversarial CAPTCHAs (AAMAS
  2026, Litman and Hajaj). Numbers from the paper's tables: EfficientNet-B7
  with FGSM needs 4.81 iterations with a random target and 2.85 with the
  class-relations target; EfficientNet-B4 noise (L2) 67.70 with FGSM and
  47.17 with the precise gradient method, random targets.
{%- endcomment -%}
<figure class="note-figure" style="--c: var(--line-adversarial)">
  <div class="fig-panels">
    <div class="fig-panel">
      <p class="fig-panel-head">1. A smarter wrong answer</p>
      <div class="fig-bars is-compact" role="list" aria-label="Steps needed to fool EfficientNet-B7">
        <div class="fig-bar-row" role="listitem">
          <span class="fig-bar-label">Random target</span>
          <span class="fig-bar-track"><span class="fig-bar is-muted" style="--w: 100%"></span></span>
          <span class="fig-bar-value num">4.81 steps</span>
        </div>
        <div class="fig-bar-row" role="listitem">
          <span class="fig-bar-label">A class it already confuses</span>
          <span class="fig-bar-track"><span class="fig-bar" style="--w: 59.3%"></span></span>
          <span class="fig-bar-value num">2.85 steps</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="fig-panel">
      <p class="fig-panel-head">2. Noise only where it counts</p>
      <div class="fig-bars is-compact" role="list" aria-label="Amount of noise added to fool EfficientNet-B4">
        <div class="fig-bar-row" role="listitem">
          <span class="fig-bar-label">Classic gradient attack</span>
          <span class="fig-bar-track"><span class="fig-bar is-muted" style="--w: 100%"></span></span>
          <span class="fig-bar-value num">67.7</span>
        </div>
        <div class="fig-bar-row" role="listitem">
          <span class="fig-bar-label">Our precise method</span>
          <span class="fig-bar-track"><span class="fig-bar" style="--w: 69.7%"></span></span>
          <span class="fig-bar-value num">47.2</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <figcaption>
    Left: steps an attack needs before EfficientNet-B7 sees the wrong class. Right: total noise (L2 distance) added to fool EfficientNet-B4. Shorter
    is better in both: a cleaner image for people that still fools the bot. From our AAMAS 2026 paper.
  </figcaption>
</figure>
